{{!-- IMAGE ZOOM — one picture, at whatever size the reader wants it, inside Foundry.

      Driven entirely by module/utils/image-zoom-window.js: the image carries no width and no
      position here, because every width it ever has is a multiple of its own natural size —
      not known until the file has loaded — and every position is where the reader put it.

      ONE root element, as AppV1 requires (activateListeners is handed the root itself, not a
      wrapper around several) — and that root is the viewport itself. No toolbar row
      (user, 2026-08-16): no magnifiers, no percentage readout, no line of instructions, so
      there is nothing for a wrapper to stack the viewport against. Wheel to zoom, drag to
      pan, double-click between fit and full size.

      The window the picture moves behind — it clips, it does not scroll (user, 2026-08-16).
      `draggable="false"` because the browser's own image drag would otherwise start the moment
      a pan does, and the reader would be dropping the file on their desktop instead of moving
      the picture. --}}
<div class="stonetop-image-zoom-view">
	<img class="stonetop-image-zoom-img" src="{{src}}" alt="{{alt}}" draggable="false">
	{{!-- An optional layer over the picture, in the picture's own coordinates. The window sizes
	      and places it exactly on top of the image on every paint, so its percentage-positioned
	      children ride the zoom and the pan while keeping their own pixel sizes. Trusted authored
	      HTML, filled through `setOverlay`, and absent entirely for every caller that only wants
	      to read a picture. A caller needing its own class here renders its own template, which is
	      what the travel maps do. --}}
	{{#if overlay}}
	<div class="stonetop-image-zoom-overlay">{{{overlay}}}</div>
	{{/if}}
</div>
